  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1118 AM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSunday remains breezy behind the front but speeds gradually taper \noff late in the day into Sunday night. The breezy northerly winds \ndoes help advect a cooler and drier airmass into the area but \ncoldest and driest air does lag a bit behind the passage of this \nboundary. The daytime highs on Sunday range from the mid to upper \n50s across the Hill Country to the 60s elsewhere. Overnight lows \ninto Monday morning are to dip into the 30s to low 40s. Monday \nremains cool with daytime highs in the 60s before southerly winds \nreturn and take over. \n\nThe southerly winds are to result in a warming trend into and \nthrough midweek, with highs returning to the 70s while overnight \nlows bounce all the way back into the 50s and 60s. Some locations \nmay even eclipse 80 degrees Wednesday and/or Thursday. Moisture \nlevels will be on the rise with the warm air advection as well, \nresulting in increasing cloud cover. This also helps to prime the \natmosphere ahead of our next storm system that comes out of the \nDesert Southwest/Four Corners region sometime late overnight \nWednesday night and Thursday. Low to medium (20-60%) rain/storm \nchances return to the forecast as a result with the best chances \nfavoring across the eastern half of the area, mainly along and to \nthe east of the US highway 281 corridor. This system seems to be \nrather progressive that could cutoff rain chances from Thursday \nnight into Friday behind a weak cold front.",
